explanation:
The definition applies the general Mathlib construction `Splitting.fromNondegComplex` to one specific simplicial object: X composed with `sigmaConst.obj R`, which turns X into a simplicial object in C. That object carries the splitting induced by the canonical splitting of X into nondegenerate simplices.

By `fromNondegComplex_f`, in degree n the map is the inclusion of the summand indexed by nondegenerate simplices, followed by P∞. This is the standard way to identify the quotient of the chains by the degenerate chains with the Moore complex inside C(X; R). The companion lemmas confirm the intended behaviour:
- composing with `toNormalizedChainComplex` gives the identity;
- composing in the other order gives P∞;
- together they form the inverse of a homotopy equivalence.

The setting is a preadditive category C with coproducts and a simplicial set X. This is the natural setting for chains of a simplicial set with coefficients in an object R. The definition does not re-implement anything: it specializes Mathlib's general construction for split simplicial objects to the simplicial-set case. That is a legitimate specific notion, not a needlessly narrow copy, so the verdict is faithful.
